    ("Functions", "What subtle bug does this code have? def add_item(item, items=[]): items.append(item); return items", "There is no bug; this works correctly every time", "The mutable default argument list is shared and persists across calls, causing unexpected accumulation of items from previous calls", "Python does not allow default arguments at all", "append() cannot be used inside a function", "B", "Default argument values are evaluated once, at function definition time, and the same list object is reused on every call that doesn't explicitly pass its own list - a classic Python gotcha."),
    ("OOP", "What is the Method Resolution Order (MRO), and why does it matter for multiple inheritance?", "It refers to the order methods are alphabetized in documentation", "It defines the specific order Python searches parent classes when resolving an attribute or method, using the C3 linearization algorithm", "It only applies to static methods", "It is unrelated to inheritance and only affects imports", "B", "When a class inherits from multiple parents, Python uses the C3 linearization algorithm to compute a consistent, predictable MRO, determining exactly which class's method gets called first in ambiguous cases."),
    ("OOP", "What is the practical effect of Python 'name mangling' on an attribute named __value inside a class?", "It has no effect; the attribute name is left unchanged", "Python internally renames it to _ClassName__value, making accidental override or access from outside less likely", "It makes the attribute a global variable", "It causes a SyntaxError at class definition time", "B", "Double-leading-underscore attributes are name-mangled to _ClassName__attr internally, which helps prevent accidental clashes in subclasses rather than providing true privacy."),
