    ("Exception Handling", "In a try/except/else/finally block, when does the 'else' clause execute?", "Only when an exception is caught", "Only if the try block completes without raising any exception", "Always, regardless of whether an exception occurred", "Only if finally is omitted", "B", "The else clause runs only when the try block finishes successfully with no exception, letting you separate 'success path' logic from the try block itself."),
    ("Generators", "What does this generator expression demonstrate about lazy evaluation? gen = (x for x in [1, 2, 3]); result = list(gen); result2 = list(gen)", "result and result2 are both [1, 2, 3]", "result is [1, 2, 3] and result2 is [] because the generator is exhausted after the first full consumption", "Both result and result2 raise an error", "result2 restarts automatically from the beginning", "B", "Generators are single-use iterators - once fully consumed, calling list() on the same exhausted generator again yields nothing, since there is no state to rewind."),
    ("Generators", "What is the purpose of 'yield from' when delegating to a sub-generator?", "It is a syntax error in modern Python", "It lets a generator delegate part of its operations to another generator, automatically forwarding its yielded values, exceptions, and return value", "It converts a generator into a normal function", "It can only be used with lists, not generators", "B", "'yield from sub_gen' transparently forwards each value yielded by sub_gen to the caller, and also properly propagates StopIteration's return value and any thrown exceptions - more than a manual for-loop with yield would handle automatically."),
    ("Iterators", "Why does calling list(some_iterator) twice on the same iterator object typically produce an empty list the second time?", "list() can only be called once per program", "An iterator maintains internal state about how far it has progressed, and once exhausted it has nothing left to yield, unlike a fresh iterable", "Python iterators are deleted automatically after first use", "This is a bug in Python's implementation", "B", "Unlike an iterable that can produce a fresh iterator each time, an iterator itself tracks its own position - once __next__() has raised StopIteration, subsequent calls continue to do so, giving no more items."),
    ("Comprehensions", "What subtle scoping difference exists between a list comprehension and an equivalent for-loop regarding the loop variable, in Python 3?", "They behave identically; both leak the loop variable into the enclosing scope", "The list comprehension's loop variable has its own local scope and does not leak into the enclosing scope, unlike a plain for-loop", "List comprehensions cannot use loop variables at all", "For-loops never expose their loop variable outside the loop", "B", "In Python 3, comprehensions execute in their own implicit scope, so the iteration variable does not leak out - unlike a regular for-loop, whose loop variable remains accessible (and retains its final value) after the loop ends."),
    ("Sets", "What is the time complexity characteristic that makes 'x in my_set' generally much faster than 'x in my_list' for large collections?", "Both have the same O(n) complexity in practice", "Set membership testing is average-case O(1) due to hashing, while list membership testing is O(n) since it must scan sequentially", "Lists secretly use a hash table internally too", "Set membership is always O(n log n)", "B", "Sets are backed by a hash table, so checking membership involves hashing the value and a near-constant-time lookup, whereas a list has no such structure and must be scanned linearly in the worst case."),
    ("Dictionaries", "Since Python 3.7, what guarantee do standard dict objects provide that earlier versions did not?", "Dictionaries became immutable", "Dictionaries guarantee insertion order is preserved when iterating over keys, values, or items", "Dictionaries can no longer have duplicate values", "Dictionary keys must now be strings only", "B", "As of Python 3.7 (and as an implementation detail in 3.6), dict iteration order reliably reflects insertion order - a behavior earlier versions did not guarantee."),
    ("Functions", "What does the following demonstrate about closures? def make_counter(): count = 0 \\n def increment(): nonlocal count; count += 1; return count \\n return increment", "The inner function cannot access or modify the outer function's variable at all", "The inner function forms a closure, and 'nonlocal' lets it modify the enclosing function's variable across multiple calls, preserving state between calls", "This code always raises a NameError", "count resets to 0 every time increment() is called", "B", "The inner function 'closes over' the outer variable count, and nonlocal explicitly allows it to modify (not just read) that enclosing variable - each call to the returned increment function continues incrementing the same persisted count."),
    ("Modules", "What problem can 'circular imports' cause in a Python project, and why?", "They are always resolved automatically with no issues", "If module A imports module B, and B imports A, one of them may be only partially initialized when the other tries to use it, causing an ImportError or AttributeError", "Circular imports are a compile-time-only concept, irrelevant to Python", "Python silently ignores the second import in a cycle", "B", "Because Python executes module code top-to-bottom on first import, a circular dependency can mean one module tries to access something from the other before that other module has finished defining it, leading to import-time errors."),
    ("OOP", "What is the practical difference between a 'classmethod' and a 'staticmethod' in Python?", "They are functionally identical in every case", "A classmethod receives the class itself as its first argument (commonly used for alternate constructors), while a staticmethod receives neither the instance nor the class", "staticmethod can modify class state but classmethod cannot", "classmethod can only be called on instances, never on the class itself", "B", "@classmethod methods take 'cls' as the first parameter and can access/modify class-level state or be used as alternate constructors, while @staticmethod methods behave like plain functions namespaced inside the class, with no automatic access to the class or instance."),
    ("Exception Handling", "Why might catching a broad 'except Exception' still be considered safer than a bare 'except:'?", "There is no difference between them", "A bare except also catches BaseException subclasses like KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit, which 'except Exception' deliberately excludes", "except Exception cannot catch built-in errors", "Bare except is actually invalid syntax in Python 3", "B", "BaseException includes things like SystemExit and KeyboardInterrupt that generally shouldn't be silently swallowed; except Exception catches ordinary runtime errors while still letting those control-flow-related exceptions propagate."),
    ("Generators", "What does adding a 'return value' statement inside a generator function actually do, given that generators use yield?", "It is a SyntaxError, since generators cannot use return", "It stops the generator and sets 'value' as the value attached to the raised StopIteration exception, retrievable via the generator's exhaustion (e.g. through yield from)", "It behaves exactly like a normal function's return, providing the value directly to a for-loop", "It restarts the generator from the beginning with a new value", "B", "In a generator, 'return value' stops iteration and attaches 'value' to the StopIteration exception's .value attribute - most visibly usable when the generator is delegated to via 'yield from', which captures that return value directly."),
    ("Python Basics", "What does Python's duck typing philosophy generally mean in practice?", "Python programs must always define explicit interfaces before use", "An object's suitability for an operation is determined by whether it supports the required methods/behavior, not by its explicit declared type", "Only built-in types can be used polymorphically", "Duck typing is unique to statically typed languages", "B", "\"If it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck\" - Python code often just calls the methods it needs (e.g. .read(), .append()) without checking an object's exact type, relying on the object simply supporting that behavior."),
    ("Comprehensions", "What performance characteristic makes a generator expression preferable to a list comprehension inside a call like sum(...) or any(...)?", "Generator expressions always execute faster per-element than list comprehensions", "The generator expression avoids allocating memory for the full intermediate collection, since sum()/any() only need to consume values one at a time", "List comprehensions cannot be passed as function arguments", "sum() and any() only accept generator expressions, never lists", "B", "Functions like sum() and any() only need to process values sequentially and can short-circuit or discard each value immediately, so a generator expression avoids the memory overhead of materializing a full list upfront."),
    ("Iterators", "What does implementing __iter__ to 'return self' in a custom iterator class assume, and what could go wrong if you need to iterate the same object twice concurrently?", "There is never any issue; objects can always be iterated infinitely many times in parallel", "It assumes only a single pass is needed; iterating the same object twice concurrently would share the same internal position, causing incorrect interleaved results", "This pattern is illegal in Python and raises a TypeError", "__iter__ returning self disables the __next__ method entirely", "B", "When __iter__ returns self, the object itself IS the iterator with one shared position counter; running two independent iterations over it at the same time would both advance and read the same internal state, producing incorrect or interleaved results rather than two independent passes."),
    ("OOP", "What is the diamond problem in multiple inheritance, and how does Python's MRO address it?", "It refers to a memory leak caused by circular references", "It occurs when a class inherits from two classes that both inherit from a common ancestor, creating ambiguity about which ancestor's method/attribute to use; Python's C3 linearization resolves this deterministically", "It only occurs in languages that don't support inheritance at all", "Python does not support multiple inheritance, so this problem cannot occur", "B", "When class D inherits from B and C, which both inherit from A, calling a method defined in A could be ambiguous about the path taken; Python's C3-linearized MRO computes a single consistent order (D, B, C, A) rather than leaving it undefined."),
    ("Modules", "What is the difference between a Python module being 'imported' versus 'executed as a script' in terms of side effects?", "There is no difference in any circumstance", "Module-level code (outside any function) runs once on first import just as it would if run directly, unless guarded by an if __name__ == '__main__' check", "Imported modules never execute any of their top-level code", "Only functions decorated with @script are ever executed on import", "B", "Python runs a module's top-level statements the first time it's imported, exactly as if it were executed directly - the __main__ guard is the conventional way to prevent script-only logic (like CLI argument parsing) from running unintentionally on import."),
    ("Exception Handling", "What does exception chaining via 'raise NewError(...) from original_error' communicate, and how does it differ from letting an exception occur naturally inside an except block?", "There is no functional or informational difference between the two", "Explicit chaining ('from') sets the __cause__ attribute intentionally, clearly signaling the new exception was deliberately raised because of the original, whereas an implicit exception during handling sets __context__ instead, signaling it was incidental", "Chaining prevents the original exception from ever being visible in tracebacks", "The 'from' clause is only cosmetic and has no effect on the exception object itself", "B", "Python distinguishes explicit causation (__cause__, set via 'raise ... from err') from implicit context (__context__, set automatically when a new exception happens to occur while handling another) - both appear in tracebacks, but they carry different semantic meaning about intent."),
    ("Lists", "Why can slicing a list (my_list[:]) be used as a quick way to create a shallow copy, and what limitation does this have for nested lists?", "It creates a fully independent deep copy with no limitations", "It creates a new outer list object, but nested mutable objects inside are still shared references, just like copy.copy()", "Slicing with [:] does not actually copy anything; it returns the same object", "Slicing only works for strings, not lists", "B", "my_list[:] builds a new list containing the same element references as the original - fine for flat lists of immutable values, but a nested list inside will still be shared between the original and the 'copy', requiring deepcopy for full isolation."),
    ("Functions", "What does Python's argument evaluation order guarantee about 'f(a(), b())', where a() and b() have side effects?", "There is no guarantee about which is called first", "Python evaluates arguments left to right, so a() is always called and fully evaluated before b() begins", "b() is always evaluated first regardless of order written", "Both are evaluated simultaneously in separate threads", "B", "Python guarantees strict left-to-right evaluation of function call arguments, so any side effects from a() will have already happened before b() starts executing."),
    ("Sets", "What does the frozenset type provide that a regular set does not?", "Faster membership testing than a regular set", "Immutability, which makes a frozenset hashable and usable as a dictionary key or as an element of another set", "The ability to store duplicate values", "Automatic sorting of its elements", "B", "Because frozenset cannot be modified after creation, it satisfies the hashability requirement needed to be used as a dict key or nested inside another set - something a mutable regular set cannot do."),
    ("Dictionaries", "What does dict.setdefault(key, default) do differently from simply checking 'if key not in dict: dict[key] = default'?", "They are functionally identical but setdefault is always slower", "setdefault performs the check-and-set in a single atomic-looking call and also returns the resulting value (existing or newly set), which is convenient for patterns like building lists inside a dict", "setdefault deletes the key if it already exists", "setdefault can only be used with integer keys", "B", "setdefault both ensures the key exists (setting it to default if missing) and returns the resulting value in one call, commonly used for patterns like 'd.setdefault(key, []).append(item)' when grouping items by key."),
    ("Strings", "Why is string concatenation using '+' inside a large loop generally discouraged compared to using ''.join(list_of_strings)?", "The '+' operator does not work on strings in Python", "Because strings are immutable, repeated '+' concatenation can create a new string object at each step, leading to O(n\u00b2) behavior in the worst case, while join() builds the result more efficiently in one pass", "join() is deprecated in modern Python", "Both approaches have identical performance in all Python implementations", "B", "Since strings can't be modified in place, each '+' concatenation in a naive loop may need to allocate and copy a growing string, whereas ''.join() is designed to compute the total size and build the result efficiently in a single pass."),
    ("Tuples", "Why can a tuple like (1, [2, 3]) still have its contents change, even though tuples are described as immutable?", "This is impossible; the tuple would raise an error if you tried", "Tuple immutability only guarantees the tuple's own references cannot be reassigned - a mutable object stored inside it, like a list, can still be mutated in place", "Tuples automatically convert any nested list into a nested tuple", "This only works in Python 2, not Python 3", "B", "Immutability of a tuple means you can't change which objects it references (or its length), but if one of those referenced objects is itself mutable (like a list), that inner object can still be modified in place."),
    ("File Handling", "What risk does opening a file with open(path, 'r', encoding=None) carry across different platforms?", "The file will always fail to open", "Python falls back to a platform-dependent default text encoding, which can cause inconsistent behavior reading the same file on different operating systems", "encoding=None automatically converts the file to binary mode", "It silently deletes non-ASCII characters", "B", "Without an explicit encoding, Python uses locale.getpreferredencoding(), which can differ between systems (e.g. Windows vs Linux), so text containing non-ASCII characters may decode inconsistently unless an encoding like 'utf-8' is specified explicitly."),
    ("Dictionaries", "Why might merging two dictionaries with 'merged = {**dict1, **dict2}' produce a different result than 'merged = {**dict2, **dict1}' when both share a key?", "Both expressions always produce identical results regardless of order", "In a dict literal, later unpacked mappings overwrite earlier ones for shared keys, so the dictionary listed last 'wins' for any duplicate key", "Dictionary unpacking with ** is not valid Python syntax", "The order only matters if the dictionaries have different lengths", "B", "When keys collide during dict-literal unpacking, whichever mapping appears later in the literal overwrites the earlier one's value for that key, so swapping the order changes which value survives."),
    ("OOP", "What does Python's abc.ABC (Abstract Base Class) module let you enforce that plain duck typing cannot?", "It has no functional effect and exists only for documentation", "It lets you define methods marked with @abstractmethod that subclasses are required to implement, raising a TypeError at instantiation if they don't", "It automatically implements every method for you", "It prevents a class from ever being subclassed", "B", "Subclassing ABC and marking methods with @abstractmethod means Python will refuse to instantiate any concrete subclass that hasn't overridden all abstract methods, giving a stronger contract than relying purely on convention."),
    ("Generators", "What memory behavior distinguishes 'send()' on a generator from a plain 'next()' call?", "send() and next() are functionally identical in every way", "send(value) resumes the generator and also passes 'value' in as the result of the current yield expression, enabling two-way communication with the generator", "send() permanently closes the generator after use", "send() can only be called before the generator starts", "B", "Unlike next() (equivalent to send(None)), send(value) both advances the generator and feeds 'value' back into the paused yield expression, letting the caller and generator exchange data rather than the generator only producing output."),
    ("Iterators", "What subtle issue can arise from writing 'for item in my_list: if condition: my_list.remove(item)'?", "This code always raises a TypeError immediately", "Removing items from a list while iterating over it can cause the loop to skip elements, since the list's indices shift as items are removed", "remove() is not a valid list method", "Python automatically creates a safe copy of my_list before every for-loop", "B", "Since iteration tracks a position index internally, removing an element shifts subsequent elements down by one, causing the next index checked to skip over what is now a different element - a classic mutate-while-iterating bug."),
    ("Modules", "What does Python's import system do differently the second time the same module is imported elsewhere in a program?", "It re-executes the module's top-level code from scratch every time", "It returns the already-cached module object from sys.modules instead of re-executing the module's code", "It raises an error, since a module can only be imported once", "It creates a completely independent copy of the module's namespace", "B", "Python caches imported modules in sys.modules; subsequent imports of the same module reuse that cached object rather than re-running its top-level code, which is why mutable module-level state persists across multiple import sites."),
    ("Sets", "Why is set iteration order not guaranteed to match insertion order, unlike Python 3.7+ dictionaries?", "Sets and dicts always have identical ordering guarantees", "Sets are implemented purely around hash-based membership testing with no ordering guarantee in the language specification, even though CPython's current implementation may appear consistent for a given run", "Sets are always sorted alphabetically", "This is a bug that will be fixed in a future Python version", "B", "Unlike dict, which explicitly guarantees insertion-order iteration since 3.7, Python's set type makes no such promise in the language spec - any apparent ordering is an implementation detail, not something code should rely on."),
    ("Functions", "What does Python's 'positional-only' parameter syntax (using a bare '/' in a function signature) let you enforce?", "It has no effect and is purely stylistic", "Parameters listed before the '/' can only be supplied positionally, not by keyword, which lets a library author change those parameter names later without breaking caller code", "It makes those parameters optional automatically", "It disables default values for those parameters", "B", "A bare '/' marks preceding parameters as positional-only, meaning callers cannot pass them by name - this protects internal parameter naming as an implementation detail that can be refactored without breaking existing callers who only ever passed them positionally."),
    ("Lambda", "Why can a lambda expression that captures a loop variable inside a list of lambdas produce surprising results, as in '[lambda: i for i in range(3)]'?", "Each lambda permanently captures the value of i at the moment it was created", "All the lambdas share the same closure variable i, so by the time they are called, i holds its final value from the loop, and every lambda returns that same final value", "This code raises a SyntaxError, since lambdas cannot reference loop variables", "Lambdas are evaluated immediately when defined, not when called", "B", "Closures capture variables by reference, not by value at creation time, so every lambda in the list looks up the same shared 'i' when finally called - since the loop has finished by then, they all see i's last value (2), not the value at each iteration."),
